Trouble Shooting

Since the process works by downloading the whole audio first, before performing transcription, I recommend you start with a small audio sample, maybe one minute
or so. This way, you will start to see the output quicker and be abe to check if anything is wrong. Initially, it can take 1 or 2 minutes to process
a 1 minute audio, load the model, and tokenize the converted wavelength data into chunks to be interpretted. So for 1 hour audios, expect more time initially 
upon startup, maybe half an hour to an hour, possibly quicker if you can programme your GPU to be involved, although this is not possible with most Macs.

Changing the checkpoint length can also be helpful in saving enough data each time if you think your device might crash or run out of battery. 
More specifics:

Stage 1 (Transcription): If it crashes here, you start over.

Stage 2 (Alignment): If it crashes during alignment, the script will reload the segments from the JSON, skip transcription entirely, and go straight to aligning.

Stage 3 (Diarization): If it crashes here, it skips transcription and alignment. This is a huge time saver for an hour-long audio.
